Track 2: Pock-a-Bye-Root
- Story & Context: We used to play Pock-a-Bye-Root in the dark, weaving our fingers through the electrified vines while hiding from the security drones in the forgotten botanical wing of the Oros Estate. My sister Elara touched the central stalk and heard the sound of rain falling inside a piano, a memory that turned her voice into the haunting cadence of a trapped bird. "Count to three before the xylem breaks" remains the warning we chant whenever the air grows too thick with the scent of ozone and rotting lilies.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Childhood silhouettes playing hide-and-seek among gargantuan, glowing rainforest roots, chiaroscuro lighting, dreamy but desolate atmosphere, 80s synth-wave visual grit.

Track 3: Carrier Frequency (Total Desiccation)
- Story & Context: The final broadcast from the Oros Estate emerged long after the botanists had vanished, leaving only a sustained tenor sax note that oscillated with the frequency of a dried-out seedpod. Survivors claimed the music wasn't recorded but generated by the sheer memory of the plants echoing through the crumbling architecture as the greenhouse shattered. "Nothing grows where the frequency kills" became the final, haunting lyric heard across the valley before the transmitters melted into slag.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A grand greenhouse shattering into millions of shards in slow motion, inside the shards are spectral images of plants growing and dying, ethereal, surrealist masterpiece.

